[{"id":123374,"sender_id":1484,"sender_full_name":"Jarek Duda","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/72aabba3c3114aa909dbdaf5e94cf2fa?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/17-n-Forum/topic/Asymmetry.20of.20Radiation.3F.20-.20in.20solutions.20of.20Maxwell.20equations/near/123374\">#1</a> Let me try to propose “T/CPT symmetry in equations vs asymmetry in solutions” type of discussion - would also gladly discuss generally, but while the main focus is usually on 2nd law of thermodynamics, I wanted to ask about looking different(?) and even more fundamental(?) but practically forgotten(?) in solutions of Maxwell equations - by Huw Price called “The asymmetry of radiation: Reinterpreting the Wheeler-Feynman argument” <a href=\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF00733218\">springer.com/article/10.1007/BF00733218</a></p>\n<p>So Maxwell equations or linearlized GR have symmetrically retarded(-) and advanced(+) solutions - based on 1979 skeptical Rosen’s <a href=\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF00759493\">“Does gravitational radiation exist?”</a>:</p>\n<p><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">□</mi><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mo>⇒</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>μ</mi><mo>±</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mo>∫</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>±</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mro