an class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.52em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">d</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">3</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathbf\">r</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.7519em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">′</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></p>\n<p>Also their convex combinations are allowed, and <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wheeler%E2%80%93Feynman_absorber_theory\">Wheeler-Feynman</a> assumed their 1/2-1/2 contributions, but e.g. inspiraling and energy loss of circulating charges/orbiting masses show there is asymmetry in solution - the actual convex combination should depend on the boundary conditions, like balance between emitters and absorbers.</p>\n<p>Seems the default assumption(?) is perfect 1-0 of only retarded, but does it have any experimental verification if not e.g. 0.99999-0.00001 instead? Theoretical arguments e.g. that emitters/absorbers balance is perfect?</p>\n<p>Where do you think this asymmetry comes from? Should it be really perfect? How to verify it experimentally? (<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2512.20692\">some proposed tests</a>)</p>","timestamp":1767766591,"subject":"Asymmetry of Radiation? - in solutions of Maxwell equations","stream_id":17,"display_recipient":"n-Forum","reactions":[]}]